    "summary": "Master why chunk size matters, chunk overlap, document-structure splitting, local embedding models, and the Model Consistency Rule.",
    "content": "### RAG-03: Text Splitting Strategies & Local Embeddings\n\n#### 1. Exam Questions & Direct Answers\n\n- **Why Chunk Size Matters:**\n  Chunk size represents the fundamental trade-off between retrieval precision and contextual completeness:\n  - **Small Chunks (100 to 200 tokens):** Maximize retrieval precision because the embedding vector represents a single tight semantic point. However, small chunks lack surrounding background context, potentially causing the model to misinterpret facts.\n  - **Large Chunks (1,000 to 2,000 tokens):** Preserve comprehensive narrative context, but the resulting embedding vector becomes diluted (averaging multiple disparate topics), reducing retrieval precision.\n  - **Standard Course Recommendation:** **256 to 512 tokens** serves as the optimal balance for general enterprise documentation.\n\n- **Chunk Overlap — What It Is and Why It Helps:**\n  Chunk overlap is the practice of duplicating a fixed number of tokens (typically 10% to 15%, e.g., 50 tokens) from the end of one chunk at the beginning of the subsequent chunk.\n  - **Why It Helps:** It guarantees that semantic statements, entity definitions, and sentences that cross a cut boundary remain complete in at least one chunk, preventing ideas from being split in half.\n\n- **Document-Structure-Aware Splitting:**\n  Instead of cutting text at fixed character counts, structure-aware splitters parse document grammar:\n  - **Markdown Splitting:** Splits at heading delimiters (`#`, `##`, `###`) to keep sections intact.\n  - **HTML / Tables:** Preserves `<table>...</table>` rows together so tabular data retains its structure.\n  - **Code Splitting:** Slices by language AST boundaries (`def`, `class`) so functions are never severed.\n\n- **Embedding Dimensionality and Model Size Trade-Offs:**\n  - **384 Dimensions (e.g., `all-MiniLM-L6-v2`):** Ultra-lightweight, executes in $<5$ ms on low-end CPUs, minimal memory footprint.\n  - **768 Dimensions (e.g., `nomic-embed-text`):** High semantic resolution, supports 8,192-token context windows, ideal for local enterprise retrieval via Ollama.\n  - **1024 Dimensions (e.g., `BGE-M3`):** High accuracy, multilingual, supports dense and sparse retrieval, but requires dedicated GPU memory and higher search latency.\n\n- **Running Embeddings Locally vs. via Cloud API:**\n  - **Local Models (Ollama / vLLM):** Zero per-token API cost, 100% data privacy (no sensitive files sent over internet), predictable latency independent of external network outages. Requires local CPU/GPU hardware.\n  - **Cloud APIs:** Zero infrastructure setup, but incurs recurring token costs, introduces internet network latency, and exposes corporate data to external third parties.\n\n- **The Cardinal Rule: The Model Consistency Rule:**\n  > **The Model Consistency Rule:** You MUST use the exact same embedding model to encode both stored document chunks and user search queries.\n  > If documents are embedded using a 768-dimensional model and queries are embedded using a 384-dimensional model, vector search fails with dimensional mismatch errors or produces completely meaningless distance scores.\n\n#### 2. Architecture & Step-by-Step Mechanics\n\n##### Comparison of Text Splitting Strategies\n\n| Splitting Strategy | Mechanism | Pros | Cons | Best Use Case |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Fixed-Size Chunking** | Slices every $N$ characters | Fast, zero complexity | Tears sentences and words mid-character | Rough prototyping, fixed log streams. |\n| **Recursive Character** | Slices by paragraphs (\\\\n\\\\n), then lines (\\\\n), then spaces | Keeps paragraphs and sentences intact | Requires separator configuration | **Standard baseline for 90% of RAG systems.** |\n| **Document-Structure Aware** | Slices along Markdown/HTML tags | Preserves tables, headers, and code | Requires structured source files | Technical documentation, API specs, tables. |\n| **Semantic Chunking** | Splits when embedding distance shifts | Pure semantic coherence | Slow (embeds every sentence) | Academic papers, complex legal contracts. |\n\n##### Leading Local Open-Weight Embedding Models Compared\n\n| Model Identifier | Dimensions | Context Window | Inference Speed | Minimum Hardware |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **`all-MiniLM-L6-v2`** | 384 floats | 256 tokens | **Ultra-Fast (<5 ms)** | Low-end CPU |\n| **`nomic-embed-text`** | 768 floats | **8,192 tokens** | Fast (10-15 ms) | Modest CPU / 4GB RAM |\n| **`BGE-M3`** | 1024 floats | **8,192 tokens** | Moderate (25 ms) | Discrete GPU / 8GB RAM |\n\n#### 3. Real Code Implementation\n\n##### Terminal Setup Commands\n```bash\n# Pull local embedding model in Ollama\nollama run nomic-embed-text\n\n# Install text splitters and dependencies\npip install langchain-text-splitters langchain-community numpy\n```\n\n##### Working Python Implementation: Recursive Splitting & Local Ollama Embeddings\n```python\nfrom langchain_text_splitters import RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter\nfrom langchain_community.embeddings import OllamaEmbeddings\nimport numpy as np\n\n# 1. Source enterprise policy document\nsource_text = \"\"\"\nACME Engineering Handbook - Policy 401: Cryptographic Key Management.\nAll cryptographic keys must be generated using an approved hardware security module.\nKeys must be rotated on a strict 90-calendar-day cycle.\nManual key entry into configuration files is strictly forbidden under compliance rule ISO-27001.\nAny detected key exposure requires an emergency rotation within 2 hours.\n\"\"\"\n\n# 2. Configure RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter\nsplitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(\n    chunk_size=160,     # Max characters per chunk\n    chunk_overlap=35,   # ~20% overlap to preserve boundary sentences\n    separators=[\"\\n\\n\", \"\\n\", \" \", \"\"]\n)\n\nchunks = splitter.split_text(source_text)\nprint(f\"Generated {len(chunks)} overlapping chunks:\\n\")\nfor i, chunk in enumerate(chunks):\n    print(f\"--- Chunk {i+1} ({len(chunk)} chars) ---\\n{chunk.strip()}\\n\")\n\n# 3. Load Local Embedding Model via Ollama (Zero cloud API fees)\nembed_model = OllamaEmbeddings(model=\"nomic-embed-text\")\n\n# 4. Embed Document Chunk and Search Query (Enforcing Model Consistency Rule!)\nchunk_vector = embed_model.embed_query(chunks[0])\nquery_vector = embed_model.embed_query(\"What is the rotation schedule for security keys?\")\n\nprint(f\"Embedding Vector Dimensions: {len(chunk_vector)} floats\")\n\n# 5. Calculate Cosine Similarity\ndef calculate_cosine_similarity(vec1, vec2):\n    return np.dot(vec1, vec2) / (np.linalg.norm(vec1) * np.linalg.norm(vec2))\n\nmatch_score = calculate_cosine_similarity(chunk_vector, query_vector)\nprint(f\"Cosine Similarity Match Score: {match_score:.4f}\")\n```\n\n##### Parameter Explanation\n- `chunk_size=160`: Maximum character length permitted in a single chunk.\n- `chunk_overlap=35`: Number of characters duplicated from the end of the previous chunk.\n- `separators=[\"\n\n\", \"\n\", \" \", \"\"]`: Priority order of split boundaries (paragraphs first, then sentences, then words).\n\n#### 4. Exam Review Points\n- **Chunk Size Trade-Off:** Small chunks = high precision, low context; Large chunks = broad context, diluted vector precision. Standard baseline is 256 to 512 tokens.\n- **Why Chunk Overlap Matters:** Prevents sentences, formulas, and entities from being severed at chunk boundaries.\n- **Model Consistency Rule:** You must use the exact same embedding model for indexing documents and querying the vector store.\n- **Local Embedding Advantages:** $0 API cost, complete data privacy, offline operation.\n"
